# TruckBill Project Instructions

## Governing Documents
Before implementation, read the relevant documents in `docs/governing/` and `docs/agent/`.

The governing documents define product requirements, architecture, business rules, design and delivery phases. Do not silently change them.

## Product
TruckBill is a unified transport, logistics, fleet management and accounting SaaS platform.

Broker, OwnFleet and Partload are integrated workflows within one company workspace, not separate applications.

Follow the approved product scope and business rules.

## Technology
Follow the existing configured stack:
- Laravel 13
- PHP 8.5
- React
- Inertia
- PostgreSQL
- Redis

Follow `Architecture.md` for the complete architecture direction.

## Architecture and Security
- Maintain modular monolith architecture.
- Enforce company and branch tenant isolation.
- Enforce backend authorization for every operation.
- Preserve financial integrity and immutable audit history.
- Never implement hard deletion or restoration contrary to governing rules.
- Follow the documented identity, ownership and permission models.

## Development Workflow
- Follow `Phases.md` as the sole delivery phase-numbering authority (DEC-001). Phase 1 is engineering foundation; initial manual Razorpay renewals are Phase 3.
- Do not start application coding — including engineering foundation implementation — until Phase 0 is formally completed and approved (DEC-004).
- Do not start business modules before engineering foundation exit criteria are met.
- Inspect existing code and conventions before changing files.
- Prefer existing dependencies and components.
- Do not change dependencies without approval.
- Test meaningful behavior changes.
- Update implementation memory and requirements traceability.
- Record significant architectural decisions as ADRs.

## Documentation
Preserve governing document intent; do not silently invent requirements.
Document conflicts and unresolved decisions as Pending instead of inventing answers.
Update progress documentation only with verified implementation results.
Governing documents remain Draft until the product owner formally approves them.

## Git Policy
- Local Git commits are explicitly permitted.
- Use small, descriptive commits after verification.
- Never execute `git push`, `git push --force`, or any equivalent remote-publishing command.
- Never publish branches, tags or releases.
- Report commit hashes and working-tree status.

## Safety
- Never expose secrets or credentials.
- Never commit environment files or private keys.
- Never modify the sibling `website/` project.
- Never modify production systems or incur external service costs without explicit approval.
- Preserve existing user changes.
